From: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: qcom: balance lane clock refcount from the disable side

Each runtime suspend/resume cycle enables the lane clocks twice but disables
them only once, so every cycle leaks one reference on each lane clock:

  resume:  ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(on, PRE_CHANGE)  enable  (link is hibern8)
           ufs_qcom_resume()                      enable  (unconditional)
  suspend: ufs_qcom_suspend()                     disable (link not active)
           ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(off)             disable (link is hibern8)  <- swallowed

The second disable is swallowed because ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks() returns
early on !is_lane_clks_enabled, which the first disable has already cleared.
Measured on a OnePlus 6 (sdm845), enable_count on gcc_ufs_phy_{rx,tx}_symbol_*
and gcc_ufs_phy_ice_core_clk goes 1 -> 5 over four forced cycles, exactly +1
each. The count therefore never returns to zero, gcc_ufs_phy_* keep gpll0 and
thence the rpmhcc XO enabled, clk-rpmh writes xo.lvl = 0x3 into the RPMH sleep
set, and qcom_stats aosd/cxsd stay pinned at 0.

Fix it from the disable side so both disables land, matching the two enables.

Note deliberately NOT taken: guarding ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks() on
is_lane_clks_enabled instead. That balances the count too, but it makes the
enable a no-op on a resume path that genuinely needs it -- the lane clocks then
stay off, ufs_qcom_resume() never completes, the host is stuck at
power/runtime_status "resuming", SCSI never calls blk_post_runtime_resume(),
and every LUN request queue stays frozen. The device becomes unbootable and
unwritable with no UIC error and no abort to show for it. Adding a disable can
only ever release a reference the driver already intended to release on this
path; skipping an enable can strand the controller.

Signed-off-by: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
---
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -344,9 +344,17 @@
 
 static void ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks(struct ufs_qcom_host *host)
 {
-	if (!host->is_lane_clks_enabled)
-		return;
-
+	/*
+	 * No is_lane_clks_enabled guard here on purpose. A suspend runs two
+	 * disables (ufs_qcom_suspend() and ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(off)) against
+	 * the two enables a resume runs (ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(on) and
+	 * ufs_qcom_resume()); guarding this side swallowed the second disable
+	 * and leaked a reference on every lane clock per cycle, which kept the
+	 * rpmhcc XO enabled and pinned xo.lvl in the RPMH sleep set. Let both
+	 * disables through so the count actually reaches zero while suspended.
+	 * clk_bulk_disable_unprepare() already WARNs rather than underflowing
+	 * if it is ever called one time too many.
+	 */
 	clk_bulk_disable_unprepare(host->num_clks, host->clks);
 
 	host->is_lane_clks_enabled = false;
